.th AC VIII 2/20/74
.sh NAME
ac \*- login accounting
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd ac
[
.bd \*-w
wtmp ] [
.bd \*-p
] [
.bd \*-d
] [
.bd \*-u
] people
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it Ac
produces a printout giving
connect time
for each user who has logged in
during the life of the current
.it wtmp
file.
A total is also produced.
.bd \*-w
is used to specify an alternate \fIwtmp\fR file.
.bd \*-p
prints individual totals; without this option,
only totals are printed.
.bd \*-d
causes a printout for each midnight to midnight
period.
.bd \*-u
prints out a summary of system down time and crashes.
Any
.it people
will limit the
printout to only the specified login names.
If no \fIwtmp\fR file is given,
.it /usr/adm/wtmp
is used.
.s3
The accounting file
.it /usr/adm/wtmp
is maintained by
.it init
and
.it login.
Neither of these programs creates the file,
so if it does not exist
no connect-time accounting is done.
To start accounting, it should be created with length 0.
On the other hand if the file is left undisturbed it will
grow without bound, so periodically any information
desired should be collected and the file truncated.
.sh FILES
/usr/adm/wtmp
.sh "SEE ALSO"
init (VIII), login (I), wtmp (V).
.sh AUTHOR
Mike Tilson modified the program to produce down time accounting.
.sh BUGS
"Down time" consists of any time during which the clock was set forward.
Therefore, if the system time is set forward by mistake,
some down time will be recorded.
It will be subtracted off when the system time is set back, but this
means that it is possible to have, say, a day with 12 hours of 
"down time" followed by a day with -12 hours of down time.
A more serious bug is that the clock continues to run when the system is
up in single-user mode.
Thus single-user time is counted as "up-time", even though it is no
good to anyone but a system hack.
